Subject: compose: the locale surface follows the launch and the host, never the seed

The timezone and the Accept-Language list were drawn by seed from a four-entry
pool in resources/profiles/catalogue.json. On a host whose real zone is
Asia/Bangkok, three pinned seeds served America/New_York, Australia/Sydney and
Asia/Singapore, and --fingerprint-explain labelled the surface `dispersion`.

That is not a narrow pool, it is the wrong mechanism. A drawn timezone does not
merely fail to match the connection's exit country, it guarantees a mismatch,
because the draw cannot see where the connection comes out: the IP geolocates
to one country and Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone names
another, and that correlation is a first-line check at every fraud vendor. The
host's own zone is strictly better than a drawn one. On a direct egress it
matches. Behind a proxy it is wrong the way a real traveller's is, rather than
wrong in a way no real machine is.

Two statements in the tree already said this should not happen.
FINGERPRINTS.md section 6 lists `locale` with "launch precedence, GeoIP" in the
conditioned-on column and gives it no option table, and the catalogue's own
policy_notes say the right widening is GeoIP-derived selection rather than a
wider pool. The generator's comment over the compiled table says it in the same
breath as contradicting itself: "Not dispersion axes -- locale is launch
precedence plus GeoIP ... but drawn by seed from the same root". The prose was
right and the code was not, and because SurfaceRecord::layer was hardcoded to
`dispersion` for both catalogue policies, the report agreed with the code
instead of the prose. Nothing in the report named the resolved zone either --
only the policy id, `en-us` -- so the one line that would have shown a Bangkok
host serving New York was never printed.

So this withdraws 0081's reasoning that the locale policy is "drawn by seed
from the same root, so the compositor needs them compiled in exactly like the
axes". Two sources remain:

  the command line, where --fingerprint-timezone and --fingerprint-locale
  arrive, and where the Python and Node packages put the answer from their
  prelaunch GeoIP lookup of the effective egress -- the proxy exit when
  --proxy-server is set, the direct IP otherwise. From the compositor's side
  those are one layer, because they arrive as the same switch; the packages
  keep them apart in their own diagnostics, which is where the distinction
  exists;

  the host, expressed as absence. A field no launch layer named is not written,
  so `locale` carries only what was named and may be absent altogether, which
  is what leaves the host's real zone in ICU (0011) and the host's real list in
  the Accept-Language pref (0013). Both already test has_value() before
  overriding, so absence needs no new code to be inherited -- it was the one
  state the draw made unreachable.

Absence is also why the two fields cannot drift apart. Neither is ever filled
in by a source that cannot answer for the other, so a host zone beside a drawn
language list -- the same defect in a smaller form -- has no way to arise.

Absence rather than the host's probed values, and the difference is not
tidiness. The obvious alternative is to probe the host's zone and language list
and write them into the profile, which looks equivalent and is not: it would
set the Accept-Language pref while leaving ICU's default locale alone, and
those are two unrelated producers. `Intl` resolves against
`Isolate::DefaultLocale()`, which is ICU's default (v8 isolate.cc:8123-8139)
and not the pref -- so a written-in host language list would have manufactured
a `navigator.languages` that disagrees with `Intl.DateTimeFormat`,
`Intl.NumberFormat`, `Intl.Collator` and every `toLocaleString`, on the one
branch that is currently correct. Writing nothing leaves both to fall back to
the same application-locale source, and they agree because they cannot do
anything else.

Measured, because that claim is the whole argument for the mechanism. The
shipped binary under `--fingerprint=host`, which composes nothing and is
therefore the shape a bare launch has after this patch, reports
`navigator.languages` en-US,en with DateTimeFormat, NumberFormat and Collator
all en-US -- on an Asia/Bangkok macOS host and on an Etc/UTC linux host alike.
The drawn pool did the opposite: en-AU,en, en-GB,en and en-SG,en language lists
sat under en-US formatting, because the application locale is pinned to en-US
by the shipped resource bundle and no seed, switch or environment variable
moves it. Three of the four pool options therefore guaranteed a disagreement a
page can read in one `toLocaleString` call, with no network request and no
reference data, which is a cheaper tell than the timezone mismatch this patch
is named for. Removing the draw removes it for every launch that names no
locale.

The voices axis follows. Its table is keyed on the resolved Accept-Language
list, and the key now comes from the composed locale or is empty, where before
it fell back to the catalogue's default list. The empty key's option set
carries no `speech` section, so a launch that names no locale keeps the host's
real speech providers. That is a real change: a bare launch used to receive a
measured voice list, and now receives the host's. It is the coherent direction.
A macOS-bundled en-US voice list under a host-owned Bangkok locale is a
contradiction, and the catalogue's policy_notes already counted the trade at
two of four locale options carrying a measured voice list. An operator who
wants the measured list passes --fingerprint-locale=en-US,en and gets it,
along with the voices table keyed on that same list.

The compiled locale policy table and kDefaultAcceptLanguages go with the draw.
Nothing in the binary selects a locale policy by id -- there is no switch for
it and this patch adds none, because making --fingerprint-locale=en-GB also
set Europe/London would be one switch overriding two fields and, worse, would
re-invent a timezone on exactly the path where a partial GeoIP answer resolved
a country but no locale. The four options keep their caller on the reference
side, where scripts/profile_resolver.py's --locale-policy selects one by id as
an internally consistent pair, and the profiles that selector can reach are
exactly the profiles --fingerprint-locale with --fingerprint-timezone can
reach, so the two implementations still compose the same set.

ComposeRequest::accept_languages, set from --lang and read by nothing, is left
alone: it was dead before this change rather than made dead by it, and a
--lang that composition ignores is its own defect to file.

Compile-unverified at the time of writing; V1 is the gate this is submitted to.
What is established is that it applies to the sequenced tree, that the draw and
the layer label it removes were read out of the applied tree rather than
inferred, and that scripts/profile_resolver.py -- the reference implementation
the golden digests compare against -- implements the same precedence and its
tests pass. The golden profile digests in scripts/test_profile_resolver.py are
stale until a build refreshes them, and report themselves so: the composition
no longer carries the `locale` and `speech` sections they were taken against.

Ledger: intl.resolved-timezone, navigator.languages, http.accept-language,
coh.timezone-network-position.

